r was born  and his mother was brazilian and  which gave him a sort of an exotic thing  which is terribly important in his  makeup  and um so there's a mixture of this  lubic merchant  his father with this woman who was born  in brazil his mother and um  so i'm standing at the house which is um  on the coast  and it's in an inlet and i'm with the um  australian  novelist richard flanagan and  the house is right on the water and  richard points out to me  and i have no idea whether this is true  or not it doesn't bother me that  in the southern hemisphere the stars are  lower  the sky is lower at night and the stars  are much more visible  and that you can in a house like that  with water so much water  you can actually read by the light of  the stars at night that starts hitting  against the water  and the water being all brightness of  course i thought this was the most  marvelous image i didn't stop to say  richard are you sure this is true  you know it's just something that thomas  man's mother tells but once once he said  it to me i was away i had the thing i  would start with  are there periods of sort of idler times  where you're waiting necessarily  for the inspiration to land  unfortunately i'm never idle  and i mean you know your job is to  finish the book  and that's the aim i mean you know so  you think well if i work today  it would be another step towards  finishing the book and so it becomes  work  you do it every day you find sometimes  that you get better at it  that the the sentences become more  fluent  or more sort of rhythmically  confident and uh but in between  and that what i do is i teach one  semester every year  so i'm i read a lot for if i'm teaching  a book i will always read the book the  days before  the class because i only work with  detail i don't have a theory  and um i find that very enriching and  and i'm not complaining about it  and i also write big long book reviews i  mean big  long book reviews and sometimes the big  book reviews  really are work and you get buried in  the business of um  whatever is your subject you know and  then so  i would have would have books piled up  to read or to consult and  i would be structuring the piece i would  be quite involved it'd be quite like  writing a novel  and the only difference is that you know  the editor becomes the person  almost like the entire reading public  where you only reaction you're ever  going to get from the editor they have  no interest in  whether you think the book is good or  bad so it's not like saying you know  this is a good review of this book it's  an essay around  the issues which the book raises and um  so it's um yeah yeah i become  really involved and with it and engaged  by it and it just  it keeps me out of harm's way how do you  find the balance between  teaching and then writing fiction the  other two terms of the year when when  you're not teaching  um i mean i think this this semester  this year  um at columbia um i taught a course on  ulysses for undergraduates  i was totally engaged with this um and  uh you know i did a lot of work every  week before the class and the classes  were busy  i think i hope by the end of the  semester the students  knew something they didn't know before  they were coming in i have a feeling  that  some of them won't forget just because  it was the book itself is so engaging  that you have that if you could just put  the energy and you get it  you get a great deal back from it and uh  that's that's how i work you were  detailed man and i've read  in preparing for this that you don't  necessarily see the importance of theory  i was wondering if you could  talk a bit more about it well i think  because i'm a novelist  um when i go into the classroom i go in  with the idea  that the book was at one point unwritten  and was written slowly and tentatively  by an  individual and that it comes from  a very enclosed and precise imagination  now once you accept this and not  everybody does  