ntury i'm interested in the idea of  how consciousness is rendered  how an individual is placed at the heart  of a book  and that often young woman but it could  be a young man  that the mind and the fate and the idea  of choice and chance  are all dealt with first of all as  unique experiences for a single  consciousness  and secondly someone living in a small  society  such as a family or a small community  and thirdly  whatever larger issues are affecting the  day  um but but the main thing is the single  face the single consciousness the single  soul as it were  and and you find this also in balzac and  you found in standard  um and you find it obviously in flow  this whole idea of  um as though you're taking your bearings  from rembrandt  or indeed from vermeer where you're  getting a single  face a single soul a single  consciousness a single set of um  feelings you're working with them so the  person  jane austen is often at her very best  when the person is alone  similarly in sort of like middle march  it's a fascinating panorama of a society  but but what really matters is when  dorothea is alone  what she does when she's alone how she  thinks and how the novel  um renders this this is a obviously the  big moment in in something like portrait  of a lady  where henry james finds it the most he  writes about the book  years later saying that they the chapter  where most happens is the chapter where  isabella's entirely alone  sitting by dying far through the night  thinking about things  and i think as a novelist i find that  those scenes are the hardest to write  and the most necessary and the ones you  have to put the most sort of  rhythmic energy into without putting too  much in  to keep the reader's mind or to keep hit  the reader's nervous system  so that the when the character is alone  that solitude has to become  energetic dynamic and dramatic in the  19th century  a novelist began to contemplate the  inner life  now it doesn't mean we don't have it  before we have it obviously in painting  we have it in shakespeare  but but i'm talking about the inner life  in a novel where the reader is reading  in silence  the reader is alone and this idea then  of looking at an individual spirit not  in relation to politics  or to history but in relation to  actually  their own feelings being enough for the  summons someone's destiny  or someone's relationship to choice or  chance being enough for a novel  without having to go into the whole  business of say the  the importance of a napoleonic war on  the novel or the importance of  corn laws or the importance of the topic  of the great reform act  these indecent george eliot are sort of  minor matters  compared to the larger question which is  that dorothea is facing her destiny  and we and and it's her life it's her  life within  that becomes dramatic now i think this  probably has  enormous political repercussions in some  way but i'm not interested in that  as much as i am in the idea of um  becoming involved in another  sensibility and the richer that  sensibility is the better as a reader  and finding that that sensibility yields  a great deal  what i mean is that that i'm that i'm  not just reading the novel for  entertainment  but i'm not reading it for instruction  either i'm  reading it for an emotional engagement  at the level of nervous system  where i'm actually feeling the thing  very deeply  the thing being what is happening to  this glittering  flickering consciousness within the  pages of this book  and the richer that this consciousness  is the more  um dynamic and the more contradictions  within the consciousness  particularly someone like madame bovary  say um or  indeed um with um something like um  dorothea or aries but archer or  um any of the women figures in them  in jane austen what's going through your  head when you're trying to  convince the reader that this is what  matters  i think sometimes that the next sentence  is a response  to the previous sentence and it may need  to be different in  tone to a different shape to it and it  may need to bring  things further but the most important  thing it needs to be true  this means it needs to be true to a  certain truth that you have set up  so that if someone is coming into a room  what they see the first thing they see  the first thing they notice  how they feel then so you do see  notice something else and make a  decision over where to move in the room  and then realize that might not have  been the right decision  and then try and move maybe further into  the shadows thus  to see further what was noticed earlier  on  so what you've built up by the end is a  picture of someone coming to a room  that um the the reader will know this  person  will want then will will i think if you  call this  if you call it the third person intimate  and if you say that the aim of it  is that very soon into a book maybe 30  pages in  and using third person singular he she  the reader starts to see the world  through that character's eyes  feel things through that character's  feelings  notice the world through that  character's ability to notice  and therefore the richer you you have  the characters  noticing skills the more developed  and the more detail you put in the more  you bring the reader in  so the reader in third person intimate  sees the world  through that um through the character's  eyes and therefore of course becomes  completely engaged with the character's  fate and worries about it  and also um i mean you you can obviously  do first person singular and some  writers do  some books some writers do and that  means you're hearing your voice  it is as though someone is whispering to  you or speaking to you or telling you  something but you're utterly alert to  their otherness to the fact that they're  another person with third person  intimate even though it should be more  remote  it oddly enough isn't that you become  engaged with the fate of the character  but  courtesy of of seeing the world  um slowly and i think it's a question of  accretion  that you build a detail you build a  detail and you make them true  or you make them seem true so the reader  doesn't stop for a second  i don't think she would feel that then  you don't want  that you want a sort of seamlessness it  doesn't mean the characters cannot  behave  out of character because it's quite  important that the characters are not  just  bland and easy to read they can be as  complex as you please  but all the time your effort is  to make what they're saying and feeling  and noticing  in some way or other true  or at least possible or at least  not irritating for the reader to say  this is an  author now the issue of the author here  is important the author has to become  invisible  the the self-suppression is supreme the  first thing you do is you say  the page is blank it is not a mirror  i am not looking at myself as i write  i'm looking at something blank i'm  filling the page  it is not me it is the character and  therefore interjecting or seeming to be  present  is is a very dangerous it can be done  but it's dangerous  and i wouldn't try it there are other  people who are very skilled at  but um all the time you're concentrating  on the person on the character you have  made  and how that character responds to  sensations or to experience  what are you looking to do with your  first edit  i think you become your own reader and  the more you read the more  books you normally just read the more  skills you become  at detecting [ __ ] at detecting a  sentence that doesn't work and detecting  something doesn't ring true  or often with me it's detecting when  something is just too long  and as each read at the moment that i  give something each read  i can take out more i work closely with  editors  and i i actually like editors and i  listen to editors  um but mainly at the moment for me it's  a question of cutting  that you just don't need a paragraph and  it takes you a while to realize i just  don't need a paragraph  it's a huge liberation just getting rid  of it and realizing oh my god i've just  freed the book from that boring  paragraph and i've released it into the  upper air  and so yeah that's what i'm that's what  i'm doing  really once the first draft is there and  if a sentence stands out i mean you're  always using repetitions  and it's always a word you use far too  much  i mean i just try to wean myself off as  as  though or as if which is you know  you find this really useful but you've  got it you use it sparingly  and they're always  so someone else can often point this out  to you do you realize how many times  and often the last sentence of a  paragraph isn't needed  that you should let the paragraph just  dangle  rather than try and do it clanging into  a paragraph  there's a lot of stuff like that um it's  quite enjoyable by the way compared to  the initial business of getting the  thing down  the editing is sort of sweet work what's  the quickest you've ever written a book  i wrote brooklyn very  quickly um i probably had it um  done in i would say  15 months maybe from it from its  original  from starting it yeah probably had it  done but  by then and um maybe with the black  water light with both of those books  others others have just been i mean  forever really you know  and something like the henry james novel  the master of  those years you know  it's lovely work and you hope it's never  going to end where do you think your  desire to write comes from i remember  meeting  sue tanzan at a literary conference in  portugal and she just said to me  without any other introduction so what  happened to you  and what she meant was what in childhood  or early adolescence what trauma or what  difficulty occurred in your life  that caused you to to move into this  sort of strange circle of wishing to  create images  um that are disguised or concealed  images of yourself  that manage actually to rel